      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "AIMS",
          "content": "Elevation of the leukocyte enzyme myeloperoxidase (MPO) in stable coronary artery disease (CAD) is controversial and its relationship with oxidized low-density lipoprotein (ox-LDL) and nitrite/nitrate (NOx) levels in CAD patients has not been evaluated. Therefore, we sought to evaluate these relationships."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MATERIALS & METHODS",
          "content": "This study included 50 stable CAD patients, 50 unstable CAD patients and 50 control subjects. Plasma MPO and ox-LDL levels were determined using enzyme immunoassay. Plasma NOx and other risk factors were also measured in the patients."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Plasma MPO levels were higher in unstable CAD patients (71.2 ± 19.6 ng/ml) than in stable CAD patients (34.5 ± 6.8 ng/ml) and control subjects (23.0 ± 3.6 ng/ml) (p < 0.001). Furthermore, plasma MPO was positively correlated with ox-LDL and negatively correlated with NOx levels (p < 0.05)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Plasma MPO levels were elevated in CAD patients and its relation to CAD may be through the oxidation of LDL and reduction of NO levels."
        }
      ]},
